while the struggle continues for a  european solution for the migrants on  lesbos the next refugee drama is now  unfolding  at another european external border the  canary islands  some 4 000 people have arrived by boats  since the beginning of the year hundreds  are thought to have died while making  the treacherous journey on the atlantic  ocean  many of them are fleeing the  deteriorating security situation in the  sahel with boats departing from morocco  and the territory of western sahara  they're also departing hundreds of  kilometers farther south from mauritania  and senegal while arrivals to  mainland spain fell by half compared to  the same period  last year landings in the canary islands  increased nearly  seven-fold the sea is surprisingly calm  this afternoon at least for weta ventura  abdul kamara and his friend ibrahim  toure remember well the high waves when  they arrived on the spanish island  they'd already spent three days on the  sea in a rubber dinghy  the atlantic crossing between africa and  the canary islands is considered the  most dangerous escape route in the world  suddenly we heard air leaking from the  rubber dinghy  at that moment everyone was scared to  death i had the feeling that this could  really be the end for us  i thought about my little sister who  would then be left without me  i thought about friends i would never  see again  [Music]  the leak was successfully patched and  abdul reached the spanish coast  in the last few weeks alone more than 1  000 migrants like him have arrived in  the canary islands  many mediterranean countries have  tightened controls against immigration  because of corona  and the well-trodden route via libya has  become dominated by criminal gangs  with such developments the dangerous  atlantic route has become increasingly  attractive  new arrivals are placed in largely empty  hotels the island's tourism association  has sharp criticism for the government's  decision  we're currently working to promote  ventura as a safe and clean island  that's in the best of health nobody  likes to see a hotel full of migrants  now  that's reality  at the government level island director  domingo fuentes  also receives criticism from other sides  human rights activists criticize that  the migrants are sealed off in hotels  and don't have access to asylum  procedures  they warn of conditions similar to those  in greece where the migrants lack the  most basic necessities  fuentes rejects the criticism  we are a southern external border of the  european union here  in the same way that the eu is currently  addressing the issues of migration from  the near and middle east  it should also address the problems  arising from migration here on the  southern border  and adding to the stress the government  sees growing concern over coronavirus  infection rates  immediately upon arrival people reaching  the island are tested  in one of the last groups half of the  tests were positive  many newcomers like kamara and today  want to leave the island as soon as  possible and move on to the european  mainland  where exactly they don't know yet the  main thing is a place where they can  find work and are safe from deportation  back home  let's go straight to fuente ventura now  in dws john phillip schultz good day to  you jan phillip spanish officials  are warning that the difficulties on  fuerteventura that we've just been  looking at will only be  increasing with the number of new  migrant arrivals set to surge  what are they expecting  yes exactly some people are already  warning that  there could be conditions like on lesbos  if the crisis continues for  too long and there are too many new  arrivals on a daily basis  the canary islands have already been a  popular  destination for migrants mainly 10  15 years ago then migration routes via  the mediterranean became  more popular but i think there are also  a lot of reasons  why the route via the canary islands is  becoming more and more popular again we  have  the problem that the conditions in libya  a typical transit country  the conditions there are horrible  migrants are kidnapped  sometimes even killed we have increased  border controls through the due to the  pandemic and  last but not least we have a lot of  desperate people in the countries of  origin especially in western africa  especially in the style  region there are more and more conflicts  and more and more desperate people  willing even to risk their lives what's  the spanish government doing to help  those  especially those whose lives become  endangered while  while making this very very difficult  crossing  not not enough at least that's what a  lot of activists say and that's also  what the human rights commissioner of  the european union  said in a state statement recently  they criticized that spain does not do  enough in the  uh search and rescue missions they also  criticize  bad housing conditions and especially  here on the canary islands a lot of  activists  criticize that migrants don't have  access to  proper asylum procedures they even speak  about deportations without a proper  legal basis and they stress the need for  more search and rescue missions briefly  if you could local politicians are  putting the blame  on the eu what do they want to see  happen  i think first of all they want more  attention of course all the focus is  on the eastern mediterranean especially  on greece  at the moment of course they also want  more financial aid from the european  union  but most of all i think they want what  most countries that have external eu  borders want  they want a european solution they don't  want to be left alone with the problem  jan phillips thanks very much for that  from fuerteventura  a police operation is now underway on  the greek island of lesbos to move  thousands of migrants and refugees  into a new temporary tent city many have  been sleeping on the streets after  a fire destroyed their refugee camp  authorities say the operation has so far  passed off peacefully but  many migrants are reluctant to move into  the new facility  rows of tents on the greek island of  lesbos  they've been set up for migrants  displaced by the fires that gutted the  maurya camp  on thursday greek police started moving  hundreds into them  over the past few days migrants had  chosen to stay on the streets in squalid  conditions  many saying they'd rather remain here  than run the risk of being detained for  months in a completely new camp  some have even returned to what remains  of moria  they said the temporary tents are not  enough they want  long-term solutions  they put us in a place where there's no  life and it's like a jungle  a human cannot live in such tents  early on wednesday only about 1 200 of  the roughly 13 000 migrants rendered  homeless by the fires  had moved to the new temporary  accommodation the un refugee agency said  there is capacity for about another 8  000 people  and had urged people to move it had said  it's the only way forward  people who have been in moriah can come  to this camp and we encourage them to  come there  because it's from here that their  process will continue  that their procedure will continue that  solutions can be found  and that people can leave the island  because  the idea is not that people remain  forever  on the island of lesvos but while  several european states have offered to  take in migrants from lesbos  others say they will not they're  offering to provide more aid  and even more tense instead  let's get the very latest now with darth  natalis a journalist  on lesbos good day to you daphne can can  you tell us  about the police operation now underway  to  move the migrants and refugees into the  new tent city that we  just saw being set up hello from the  island of lesbos  yes indeed there was a police operation  that started very early in the morning  uh right before uh dawn actually police  forces  uh brought riot buses and they blocked  off  the road where the people have been  staying for the past eight  or nine days uh they blocked off the  road so that  people would not leave and sort of  contain them in this area where they  have been staying  already all these days uh there's the  traffic has been  disrupted since uh since then since the  people are living here on the streets  the shops on the area some a couple of  supermarkets  and gas stations have been they cannot  they cannot operate so since this  morning  the police went around to people and  and told them that they will have to go  to the new camp  to access facilities there water  electricity they will be registered  there in the new site again they will be  handed  non-food items sleeping bags tents  a smaller test actually because there  are tents there  and other non-food items that they that  the agencies the aid agencies are  providing  and uh there's at the moment the latest  that i have is that  there's 2 300 people around 2  300. okay i want to get to that question  how many people are not going into the  camps and how many have already  moved into the new facilities  so um the latest information is that  at least 2 300 people have  gone since uh yesterday morning in  in the camp the operation started seven  in the morning very early  uh where peop where police authorities  were waking up people telling them that  they will have to pack their stuff  and slowly head to the camp it's not a  it's not a big distance  to walk or over there and there's still  many people that  are reluctant to go to the new facility  they  they think or they have heard that there  is not  there's no water they told me that they  think that there's no electricity  or that they're going to be closed in  the camp and will not be able to  to come out okay there's  also um we have to leave it there daphne  thanks so much for bringing us up to  date  on the situation right now uh at the  moria camp there  right outside it on lesbos thanks very  much  well the european parliament's due to  discuss the situation on lesbos during  today's debate our correspondent  barbara basil standing by for more on  that barbara  can we expect the eu to agree to a  common approach to  resolving the situation on lisbos  there is a no wonder no common approach  because as we know this is the most  poisonous political battleground in the  european union so  even when this debate was scheduled in  the european parliament there were some  right-wing and some populist parties who  said  we don't want this debate uh you know  where is the problem it was the refugees  themselves who are supposed to have put  the camp  set the camp on fire and so we we don't  want to talk about this  but we just of course the debate is  taking place there is a majority against  that very hard line  uh position but it shows how deep the  divisions go  we just heard the the european  commissioner ilv johansen  the commissioner for refugees and she  said progress on maurya  is slow and that is like we just saw  people  mistrust the the the local  the police and the people on the ground  and and they say we don't want to go in  those camps anymore because we'll  be stuck there forever and after five  years of people being stuck in moria  no wonder so solutions here are  difficult at the moment  the discussion just goes about getting  more humanitarian aid there  and there is no real long-term or even  medium-term solution inside it seems  okay we're focusing right now on moria  but greece is not the only country with  an eu extra  external border that's currently  struggling with an influx of migrants  absolutely not nobody's talking about  spain because the the southern europe uh  southern mediterranean route  has been sort of come back to life  towards the end of last year  people mostly from the sahel area in  conflict  conflict zones down there like mali uh  people from cameroon and so on  they've been moving up through morocco  and and they have now  ended up on the on the canary islands  and it's the same situation  we don't have exact figures but it's  several thousand people that  have arrived there already and uh it's  the same situation as in greece  the islands are a bottleneck and the  greek  the the greek authorities are not the  spanish authorities are not willing or  not capable of moving people on from  there  same situation again because they say  nobody in europe is taking these  refugees off our hands  there is no redistribution and that is  still the point we're stuck at  barbara thanks so much for looking into  this as that debate continues today  in uh brussels thank you very much

